A||N||8||Songman||88 Fiero Help||||5||
Z||000000||Songman||08-29-2000||08:53 PM||||I really need help on this one folks. I recently bought my girlfriend a nice little 88 coupe with a 4 cylinder automatic. Everything was going along fine and then all of a sudden it backfired and started running really rough. Where it gets weird is that if I shut off the car and restart it, sometimes it runs correctly again. It is sorta like rolling the dice everytime you start the car. I have figured out that it will always backfire and start running rough if I punch it.<BR>Here is part two of the problem. So far I have replaced the ECM, O2 sensor, plugs, wires, and the crank sensor. I am at a loss. The fact that this is an intermittent problem makes me think electrical, but all of that has been replaced. I say all of it... I was told that the coil packs would not cause this problem so I didn't replace them.<BR>I could really use some help on this so please feel free to e-mail me if you have any idea what I am up against. My girlfriend wants her car back! Thanks...||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrfiero||08-29-2000||09:48 PM||||Double check the plug wires.....the DIS ignition system used on the '87-'88 4-cyl. is tempermental.  Did you replace the coils with the module?  That's another item I'd look at.<P>If all else fails, you aren't that far from The Fiero Factory (you're about an hour away).......bring it to them and they'll have you fixed up in no time!  They are located just north of Huntsville, AL at 8710B Hwy 53, Toney,AL. <P>E-mail them at   fierofactory@juno.com||||reg||6
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||08-30-2000||08:59 AM||||How many miles? If it's over 50k and has the factory exhaust on check the converter and replace it and the muffler if broken down (converter). A good back fire and abnormal running conditions following that is a good indication. A bad coil on the ignition module can cause this as well, but not normally an 'intermintant' problem. When bad, stays bad 90% of the time.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Songman||08-30-2000||09:59 AM||||Thanks to both of you for your responses. I will check both things today. The car has 118k on it so I'm sure it needs all of this stuff anyway.<BR>I think I will go ahead and replace the module and both coils. That way the entire ignition system will be new.<BR>I did notice yesterday that the exhaust manifold has a small crack in it. Guess I might as well replace the entire system because it does look like original. Any suggestions to get the most out of this 4-banger?<BR>Thank you all very much!<p>[This message has been edited by Songman (edited 08-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Songman||08-30-2000||09:59 AM||||Thanks to both of you for your responses. I will check both things today. The car has 118k on it so I'm sure it needs all of this stuff anyway.<BR>I think I will go ahead and replace the module and both coils. That way the entire ignition system will be new.<BR>I did notice yesterday that the exhaust manifold has a small crack in it. Guess I might as well replace the entire system because it does look like original. Any suggestions to get the most out of this 4-banger?<BR>Thank you all very much!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Songman||08-30-2000||10:04 AM||||Sorry about the double response. I tried to catch it and delete the 'quoted' part. I guess I'm still new at this....||||reg||1
Z||000006||Songman||09-01-2000||10:48 PM||||Thanks to mrfiero, Indiana_resto_guy, and Ed Parks at The Fiero Factory! I replaced the coils and module today and the little 4-cylinder is purring like a kitten. Finally, my girlfriend is happy again!<P>Now I have to get the 85 GT that I jsut bought home from SC and get to work on it! I'll see you soon, Ed!||||reg||10
Z||000007||theogre||09-02-2000||01:53 PM||||take the plugs out and check the gap. They need to all be as close to 060 as posible. Also, stay with standard plugs. Some of the "improved" plugs and DIS don't get along. (Bosh platinums are a good one to die when put in DIS motors.)<P>Also, go into my cave and read the notes on DIS if you didn't already. If you put the brick back onto the engine block without sealing the bolt threads you will leak oil.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Songman||09-02-2000||02:07 PM||||Uh-oh! I didn't do that.... I just stuck the bolts back in! What exactly do I seal them with? Thanks for that advice, Ogre.||||reg||11
